WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.500
"Why People Gain Weight in the Fall"

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.420
SAD doesn’t just stand for
standard American diet.

00:00:14.429 --> 00:00:17.570
There’s a condition known as
seasonal affective disorder

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:20.420
that’s characterized by increased
appetite and cravings,

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:23.880
along with increased sleepiness and
lethargy that begins in autumn

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:26.790
when light exposure starts to dwindle.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:30.399
This now appears to just
represent the far end

00:00:30.399 --> 00:00:33.340
of a normal spectrum
of a human behavior.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.400
We all appear to eat more
as the days get shorter.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.800
There"s a “marked seasonal rhythm” to
calorie intake with greater meal size,

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:46.190
eating rate, hunger, and overall
calorie intake in the fall.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:50.070
In preparation for winter, some animals
hibernate, doubling their fat stores

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:54.780
with autumn abundance to deal with
the subsequent scarcity of winter.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:00.540
Genes have been identified in humans
that are similar to hibernation genes,

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:04.890
which may help explain why we
exhibit some of the same behaviors.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:07.470
The autumn affect isn’t subtle.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:10.350
Researchers calculated a
222-calorie difference

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.440
between calorie intake
in the fall versus spring.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.300
And this isn’t just because it’s colder,

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.840
since we eat more in the
fall than the winter.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.810
It appears we’re just
genetically programmed

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:26.139
to prep for the deprivation of
winter that no longer comes.

00:01:26.139 --> 00:01:30.560
It’s remarkable that in this day and
age of modern lighting and heating

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.749
that our bodies would still pick
up on the environmental cues

00:01:33.749 --> 00:01:36.159
of the changing seasons enough to affect

00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:39.420
such a major influence
on our eating patterns.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.170
Unsurprisingly, bright light
therapy is used to treat

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:46.490
seasonal affective disorder,
nearly tripling the likelihood

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.579
of remission compared to placebo.

00:01:48.579 --> 00:01:53.700
Though it’s never been directly tested,
it can’t hurt to take the dog out

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.079
for some extra autumn morning
walks to maybe help fend off

00:01:57.079 --> 00:01:59.689
some of the coming holiday
season weight gain.

00:01:59.689 --> 00:02:03.060
It’s funny: people blame the
holidays for overeating,

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.069
but maybe instead of the holidays
causing heightened intake,

00:02:07.069 --> 00:02:09.381
maybe the heightened intake
that goes with the fall

00:02:09.381 --> 00:02:13.819
caused the holidays to be scheduled
then in the first place.

00:02:13.819 --> 00:02:16.228
Regardless… other “specific
recommendations

00:02:16.228 --> 00:02:20.580
for the prevention of obesity…by
improving the circadian system health,”

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:23.120
based on varying degrees of evidence

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:27.030
include sleeping during the night
and being active during the day.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:30.380
Sleep enough, at least 7
or 8 hours a night.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:33.740
Early to bed, early to rise.
Short naps are fine.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:38.420
Contrary to popular belief,
daytime napping does not appear

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:40.480
to adversely impact sleep at night.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:46.120
Avoid bright light exposure at night and
sleep in total darkness when possible.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.989
Make breakfast or lunch your
biggest meal of the day,

00:02:48.989 --> 00:02:52.484
don’t eat or exercise right before bed,

00:02:52.484 --> 00:02:58.220
and avoid eating at night completely.

